---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {farmModel} from '../../dossiers/farm-households.model.js';
import {farmChart,farmNumbers} from '../../dossiers/farm-households.charts.js';
const D=bySlug['farm-households'],model=farmModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const N=farmNumbers(model);
---
<Base title="Farm households · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Farm livelihoods</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/farm-households/" ogImage="/og/farm-households.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="farm-households-nss77-24, farm-households-nss77-25, farm-households-report-29" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">An Indian farm family lives on more than its crop. Wages, milk, a small shop and rent from leased-out land all add up, and the smaller the farm, the more the family leans on work outside it. A national survey of farm households counts every one of these sources, along with the land, the debts and the risks behind them.</p></div>
 <div class="meta"><span>Income over <b>{model.periods.annual}</b></span><span>Loans as at <b>{model.periods.debt}</b></span><span>Survey <b>Situation Assessment of Agricultural Households, NSS 77th round</b></span></div>
 <div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Monthly income of a farm household</div><div class="v acc" id="n-paid_income">{N["n-paid_income"]}</div><div class="d">Average, after the cash it spent on farming</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Counting the family’s own labour and inputs</div><div class="v" id="n-imputed_income">{N["n-imputed_income"]}</div><div class="d">The same income, with own inputs valued</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Average outstanding loan</div><div class="v" id="n-debt">{N["n-debt"]}</div><div class="d">Across all farm households, borrowers or not</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Farm households in debt</div><div class="v" id="n-indebted">{N["n-indebted"]}</div><div class="d">With any loan outstanding</div></div></div></div>
 <section data-inputs="farm-households-nss77-24, farm-households-nss77-25" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>A farm is one income among several</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Picture a family in eastern Uttar Pradesh with less than a hectare. It grows paddy in the monsoon and wheat in winter, keeps a buffalo for milk, and one son works on construction sites in the town for part of the year. The crop brings money twice a year, at harvest; the wages and the milk arrive every week.</p><p>For a family like this, how much the land yields matters less than whether there is work nearby and what the milk fetches. Only on larger holdings does the crop become the main income.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Farming also has costs that never show up as cash. The family’s own labour, seed kept back from last year’s harvest, fodder grown on its own land: all of these have a value. Counting them changes how much farming really earns, especially for animals, where family members do most of the work.</p><p>Debt is the other side. Seed, fertiliser and a pump set are bought before the harvest pays for them, and the money often comes from a bank, a co-operative or a moneylender.</p></div></div></section>
 <section data-inputs="all farm-households bundles; Report 587 definitions" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read the survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Who counts as a farm household.</b> The survey counts a rural household as agricultural if it earned more than <b id="n-qualifying_produce">{N["n-qualifying_produce"]}</b> from farming in the last <b id="n-definition_days">{N["n-definition_days"]}</b> days and at least one member worked on the farm on their own account. Families of farm labourers with no farm of their own are not included.</p><p><b>Monthly averages over a year.</b> Income, costs and investment are averages per household per month, across the agricultural year from July to June. A family’s cash comes in lumps, at harvest and on pay day; the average smooths those out.</p><p><b>Two seasons, two visits.</b> Survey staff visited each household twice: once for the monsoon (kharif) season and once for the winter (rabi) season. Crop questions use the season of the crop, so paddy figures come from the first visit and wheat from the second.</p><p><b>Check who is in the base.</b> Income and debt are averaged over all farm households. Crop costs use only households that grow crops; animal receipts only those that keep animals; sales only those that sold. Each chart says which.</p><p><b>Land possessed.</b> Land a household owns or rents in and can farm, less the land it rents out.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">An average, not a typical family</span>A few large farms pull the averages up. The typical farm family earns less than the average shown.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">One survey year</span>This survey covers {model.periods.annual}. It cannot show a trend on its own, and its rupee figures are at the prices of that year.</aside></div></div></section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="facts-farm-households; all analytical bundles" data-prose="data-bound snapshot facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the survey found</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{model.periods.annual}</span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>The average farm household earned <b id="n-wage_income">{N["n-wage_income"]}</b> a month from wages and <b id="n-crop_income">{N["n-crop_income"]}</b> from its crops. Land makes the difference: households with ten hectares or more earned <b id="n-top_land_income">{N["n-top_land_income"]}</b> a month, those with the smallest plots <b id="n-small_land_income">{N["n-small_land_income"]}</b>. And <b id="n-under_hectare_pct">{N["n-under_hectare_pct"]}</b> of farm households possess less than a hectare.</p><p>Most paddy is sold to traders in the local market. Only <b id="n-msp_aware_paddy">{N["n-msp_aware_paddy"]}</b> paddy sellers knew the minimum support price, and fewer still sold at it. Insurance is rare: <b id="n-not_insured_paddy">{N["n-not_insured_paddy"]}</b> paddy growers had not insured their crop.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Commercial banks hold <b id="n-bank_debt_pct">{N["n-bank_debt_pct"]}</b> of what farm households owe. Moneylenders hold <b id="n-moneylender_debt_pct">{N["n-moneylender_debt_pct"]}</b>, more than co-operatives and regional rural banks combined.</p><p>Debt rises with land. A household with more land is both more likely to borrow and able to borrow much more, because land is what banks lend against.</p></div></div></section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.bundles))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>{N['pv-'+p.id]}</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone" id={'plot-'+p.id} set:html={farmChart(p,model)} /><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">MoSPI, NSS 77th round, Situation Assessment of Agricultural Households. {p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_587m_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div></div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all farm-households bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"Farmers live off their crops."</b> For the average farm household, wages bring in more than crops. Crops become the main income only on larger holdings.</p><p><b>"Every rural family is a farm family."</b> The survey leaves out households of farm labourers with no farm of their own, artisans and others. Their incomes are not in these figures.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"The average income is what a typical farm family earns."</b> A small number of large farms lift the average well above what most families earn.</p><p><b>"Knowing the MSP means getting it."</b> Many more sellers knew the support price than sold to the agencies that pay it.</p><p><b>"Moneylenders are gone."</b> They still hold a large share of what farm households owe.</p></div></div></section>
 <EveryCut slug="farm-households"/><GuideData slug="farm-households"/>
 <section data-inputs="farm-households source catalogue and report" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources and method</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>Every figure comes from MoSPI’s Situation Assessment Survey of Agricultural Households, NSS 77th round, with the land and livestock survey run alongside it. The charts above answer one question each; every published table and cut, state by state, is in the table above and the downloads. Where MoSPI’s online tables are wrong (the debt table repeats one state’s figures under every state, and the support-price and insurance tables carry muddled labels), the guide uses the matching tables of the printed report instead.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_587m_0.pdf">MoSPI: the survey report, with definitions, detailed tables and sample sizes</a>. <a href="/guides/farms/">Farms</a> covers national crop production; <a href="/guides/households/">Household assets and borrowing</a> covers the separate debt and investment survey.</p><p>Data revision date: <b id="prov-vintage">{model.plates[0].vintageText}</b>; MoSPI does not publish one for its online tables. Report published <b id="prov-edition">{model.plates[0].reportEditionDate}</b>; online tables copied by the Atlas on <b id="prov-capture">{model.plates[0].archiveCapturedDate}</b>. <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1753856&amp;lang=2&amp;reg=48">Official release</a>.</p></section>
</div>
<script>import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>

